**
** ============================================================
** PART DEFINITIONS
** ============================================================
**
** Each *Part defines geometry, mesh, sections, and orientations
** independently before being assembled later.
**

*Part, name=concrete
** Import the concrete mesh from an external input file
*Include, input="./mesh/concrete.inp"

** Assign a solid (continuum) section to the concrete elements
**  - elset=concrete : element set defined in the included mesh
**  - material=concrete : material defined later
*Solid Section, elset=concrete, material=concrete
*End Part

** ------------------------------------------------------------

*Part, name=steel
** Import the steel mesh (plate + anchor)
*Include, input="./mesh/steel.inp"

** Assign material to the steel plate elements
*Solid Section, elset=PLATE, material=STEEL_PLATE

** Assign material to the steel anchor elements
*Solid Section, elset=ANCHOR, material=STEEL_ANCHOR

** Fixture: flat washer (DIN 125A / ISO 7089) and hex nut (ISO 4032 / DIN 934)
** Separate material so the fastener stiffness can be varied independently
*Solid Section, elset=WASHER, material=STEEL_FASTENER
*Solid Section, elset=NUT, material=STEEL_FASTENER

** Define a cylindrical local coordinate system
** This is later used for the cohesive (mortar) behavior
*ORIENTATION, name=MORTAR_CYL, SYSTEM=CYLINDRICAL
0, 0, 0,   0, 1, 0
**
** First point: origin of the coordinate system
** Second point: direction of the cylinder axis

** ------------------------------------------------------------
** Cohesive section for the mortar layer
** ------------------------------------------------------------
**  - elset=MORTAR : cohesive elements
**  - material=stick_slip : traction–separation law
**  - response=TRACTION SEPARATION : cohesive formulation
**  - ORIENTATION=MORTAR_CYL : defines normal and shear directions
**  - STACK DIRECTION=ORIENTATION : thickness direction follows orientation
*COHESIVE SECTION, elset=MORTAR, material=stick_slip, response=TRACTION SEPARATION, ORIENTATION=MORTAR_CYL, STACK DIRECTION=ORIENTATION
*End Part

**
** ============================================================
** ASSEMBLY
** ============================================================
**
** The assembly instantiates parts and defines interactions
**

*Assembly, name=Assembly
** Create an instance of the concrete part
*Instance, name=concrete-1, part=concrete
*End Instance
** Create an instance of the steel part
*Instance, name=steel-1, part=steel
*End Instance
** ------------------------------------------------------------
** TIE CONSTRAINTS
** ------------------------------------------------------------
** Tie constraints enforce perfect bonding (no slip, no separation)
** between specified master and slave surfaces.
** First surface listed is the slave, second is the master.
**
** Tie nut to anchor shaft (represents the engaged thread; threads not modelled)
** Tie nut to washer
** Tie washer to plate (fully clamped fixture: no slip under the washer)
** Tie anchor to mortar
** Tie mortar to concrete
**
** The anchor shaft is not tied to the plate hole: the shaft/hole pair is left in
** contact so that bearing of the shaft on the hole is resolved rather than
** imposed (see the contact section below).
** Slave is the shaft surface: it is the more finely meshed side of this pair
** (96 vs 48 faces, since the nut is meshed coarsely on purpose).
*TIE, NAME=NUT_TO_ANCHOR, ADJUST=NO, TYPE=SURFACE TO SURFACE
steel-1.anchor_to_nut, steel-1.nut_to_anchor
**
** POSITION TOLERANCE covers the DIN 125 clearance bore: the washer top starts
** at r = dWasherIn/2 while the nut bottom starts at r = dAnchor/2, so the
** innermost ring of nut-bottom slave nodes would otherwise find no master
** facet. Scale with dAnchor: > (dWasherIn - dAnchor)/2 = 0.5 mm for M16.
*TIE, NAME=NUT_TO_WASHER, ADJUST=NO, POSITION TOLERANCE=0.6, TYPE=SURFACE TO SURFACE
steel-1.nut_to_washer, steel-1.washer_to_nut
**
** Washer bottom tied to the plate top: the fixture is fully clamped, so no slip
** and no separation are allowed under the washer. No POSITION TOLERANCE is
** needed here - both surfaces sit at y = tPlate and both are bored to
** dClearance, so every slave node has a master facet directly beneath it.
** Slave is washer_bottom: it is the smaller surface (54 faces against 198) and
** marginally the finer of the two.
*TIE, NAME=WASHER_TO_PLATE, ADJUST=NO, TYPE=SURFACE TO SURFACE
steel-1.washer_bottom, steel-1.plate_top
*TIE, NAME=ANCHOR_TO_MORTAR, ADJUST=NO, TYPE=SURFACE TO SURFACE
steel-1.mortar_to_anchor, steel-1.anchor_to_mortar
*TIE, NAME=MORTAR_TO_CONCRETE, ADJUST=NO, TYPE=SURFACE TO SURFACE
concrete-1.concrete_to_mortar, steel-1.mortar_to_concrete
*End Assembly
